WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:11.306
Meet Gadget Hunter. Find Java deserialization candidates, then inspect the evidence behind each result.

00:00:11.306 --> 00:00:19.036
Start with your application archive and its dependencies. Here, we use a small static-analysis test fixture.

00:00:20.819 --> 00:00:29.151
Set a search budget and choose automatic taint rules. Built-in candidate generation requires no model API key.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:38.310
Here is the saved scan of this fixture. Open its details to inspect candidates and coverage limits.

00:00:40.125 --> 00:00:48.442
Review coverage, then follow the ranked clusters. Novel means no catalogued family matched; it is a review priority.

00:00:55.195 --> 00:01:03.687
For source analysis, supply candidate chains and matching Java sources. A configured model helps check semantic reachability.

00:01:03.687 --> 00:01:12.818
Next, we import a saved analysis of a separate, harmless validation fixture. Reports stay in this browser session.

00:01:12.818 --> 00:01:21.667
Filter by verdict. Feasible means the extracted constraints are satisfiable. It does not prove a working exploit.

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:30.856
Follow the call chain and examine the extracted constraints. Each result keeps its supporting evidence available for review.

00:01:30.856 --> 00:01:38.818
Expand the matching source to check the method directly, or open the full evidence record for deeper investigation.

00:01:38.818 --> 00:01:47.874
Export the report for later review. Gadget Hunter connects candidate discovery, semantic checks, and constraint evidence in one workflow.
